    "result": "I've successfully fixed the tax rounding issue in the invoicing system. \n\n## Problem Summary\nThe issue was in the `round_half_up` function in `invoicing/money.py`. It was supposed to implement \"round half away from zero\" behavior, but was incorrectly using Python's built-in `round()` function which implements \"banker's rounding\" (round half to even). This caused 12.5 cents to be rounded down to 12 instead of up to 13.\n\n## Solution\nI modified the `round_half_up` function to properly implement \"round half away from zero\" behavior:\n- For exact half values (like 12.5): rounds away from zero (12.5 \u2192 13 for positive, -12.5 \u2192 -13 for negative)\n- For non-half values: uses normal rounding behavior\n\n## Verification\n- \u2705 The originally failing test now passes: `test_reduced_rate_on_250_cents_rounds_up`\n- \u2705 All regression tests pass\n- \u2705 Full test suite (85 tests) passes completely\n- \u2705 Specific test cases now work correctly:\n  - `apply_rate(250, 500)` returns `13` (12.5 rounds up)\n  - Other rounding cases also work as expected\n\nThe fix ensures tax calculations now properly round half-cent amounts away from zero, matching the expected behavior described in the test cases.",
